  "notes": "West leads the ♥4 against 7♥. West's heart lead is won in dummy as East discards. South's only possible losers are in spades. Taking the spade finesse is one alternative.\n\nSouth also might think about discarding the ♠Q on his third club. South might later be able to crossruff. This line of play will fail if West has only 2 clubs.\n\nSouth's best chance is to look at the problem from dummy's point of view. Dummy has 3 diamond losers that can be ruffed with high trumps in South's hand. After drawing trump, dummy's ♠Q can later be discarded on South's third club.\n\nSouth starts by cashing a top diamond and playing a small diamond, ruffing with a high trump. Dummy can be entered in clubs and another diamond is ruffed high. A spade is played to the ♠A and dummy's last diamond loser is ruffed high.\n\nSouth still has a trump to get to the dummy. West's remaining trumps can now be drawn. The last 3 tricks will be taken by the ♦K and South's 2 good clubs.\n\nThe technique of setting up the short trump hand is known as a dummy reversal. A good habit to get into before playing to trick 1 is to look at the contract from both the declarer's and the dummy's point of view.",
